    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d102">

                <pb facs="0186" n="154" sameAs="frus:frus1955-57v13/pg154"/>

                <head>102. Telegram From the Department of State to the Embassy in the United
                        Kingdom<anchor n="1" xml:id="d102-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1957-07-31T19:07:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">July 31,
                                1957—7:07 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>942. Deptel 884 to London, 192 to Amman.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d102-f2"/>
                    Following points will be made in conversation with UK Embassy August 1 and
                    should be made same day to FonOff:<anchor n="3" xml:id="d102-f3"/>
                    <list>
                        <label>1)</label>
                        <item>US pleased receive information re UK decision on interest-free loan to
                            Jordan.</item>
                        <label>2)</label>
                        <item>US recalls that joint Anglo-American assessment Jordan situation
                            towards end June agreed that preservation Jordan as independent
                            pro-Western state of highest import Anglo-American policy and that
                            Jordan non-viable and must have outside support to extent of minimum £15
                            million annually.</item>
                        <label>3)</label>
                        <item>US has made heavy financial commitments to Jordan in recent months. US
                            hopes that UK in spite of change in its position in Jordan will in light
                            of its interest in strengthening pro-Western forces in Near East give
                            earliest consideration to making available larger measure of assistance
                            than now proposed. Continuation of economic assistance at levels of
                            recent years prior to termination treaty would be most helpful.
                            Postponement of annual Jordan debt repayment is among other assistance
                            measures which might be considered. US realizes difficulties both
                            political and economic involved for UK in connection such possible steps
                            but believes US–UK <pb facs="0187" n="155" xml:id="pg155"/> objectives
                            in Jordan so important as to warrant special efforts by both
                            countries.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Herter</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d102-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d102-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, 885.10/7–3157. Secret; Priority.
                    Drafted by Rockwell and Shaw and approved by Berry who signed for Herter.
                    Repeated to Amman and pouched to Baghdad, Jidda, Cairo, and Damascus.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d102-f2" n="2" xml:id="d102-f2-content">
                <p>On July 29, the Department in telegram 192 to Amman, repeated to London, informed
                    the Embassy, among other things, that the United Kingdom agreed to give Jordan
                    an interest free loan amounting to £1,130,000. (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Ibid.,</hi> 885.10/7–2957)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d102-f3" n="3" xml:id="d102-f3-content">
                <p>A memorandum of the conversation by Bergus is not printed. (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Ibid.,</hi> 885.10/ 8–157)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
